  the radius mismatch, and which way it runs.  The Wolf estimator's sigma is the luminosity-weighted GLOBAL one; the three sparse clusters have exactly ONE measured bin each, and it does not sit at r_h,l:
     NGC 2419   measured at  19.8 pc = 1.00 r_h,l
     Pal 3      measured at  11.1 pc = 0.55 r_h,l
     Pal 4      measured at  13.1 pc = 0.83 r_h,l
     Pal 14     measured at  27.5 pc = 1.00 r_h,l
     a dispersion measured INSIDE r_h,l is biased HIGH relative to the global one, by at most the models' own central-to-global ratio 1.12 (nothing can be more centrally weighted than the centre).  A high sigma_obs makes the framework look BETTER, so correcting for this makes the liability worse, not better.
  [PASS] 93j2 AGAINST INTEREST -- the one geometric systematic that has been left uncorrected runs in the framework's favour, and correcting it at its maximum defensible size makes the over-prediction larger.  The liability is therefore quoted at its SMALLEST, with the correction named and not applied   (over-prediction in the three sparse clusters 1.79x as quoted, 2.01x if every measured sigma is corrected to a global one at the maximum ratio 1.12)
    with the LOWER published dispersions where they exist (Jordi+2009, Frank+2012): over-prediction [1.09, 2.49, 3.99] (log-mean 2.21x) -- the framework fares worse, not better
  [PASS] 93j the liability is robust to every systematic that could plausibly move it.  The external-field boost falls only as M_MW^(-1/4), so even giving the Milky Way a 1e12 Msun DARK-halo mass (which the framework does not have) leaves an over-prediction; a 15% distance error moves it by 15%; adopting the lower published dispersions makes it worse; and binaries inflate the measurements, so the true discrepancy is larger   (over-prediction at M_MW = 1e12 Msun is still 1.22x; at the framework's own 6e10 it is 1.79x)

