  [PASS] 93-M1 a_0 -> 0 (nu == 1) must collapse the framework's prediction exactly onto the Newtonian one, and the live boosts must be far from 1 -- otherwise the estimator is returning the Newtonian answer by construction   (nu=1 gives [4.676, 0.743, 0.998, 0.6] == Newtonian [4.676, 0.743, 0.998, 0.6]; live boosts [1.284, 2.496, 2.169, 2.524])
  [PASS] 93-M2 shuffling which cluster's measured dispersion belongs to which cluster's photometry and structure must DESTROY the Newtonian M/L consistency -- if it does not, that consistency was never in the data   (true Newtonian M/L scatter 0.317 dex sits at the 8th percentile of 4000 shuffles (median shuffled scatter 1.042 dex))
  [PASS] 93-M3 a_0 scanned DOWN by 1e4 must send the boost smoothly to 1 (the framework's Newtonian limit) and a_0 scanned UP must make the over-prediction worse -- both directions, so that no threshold was tuned   (boost at a_0/1e4 = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]; predictions at 10 a_0 rise by [1.54, 1.73, 1.71, 1.73]x)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- item 93
======================================================================================================================
  LIABILITY, and a known one, but smaller and differently located than the item supposed.
  (1) The item's premise is wrong in detail: only Pal 14 is external-field dominated.  Pal 3 and Pal 4 are isolated
      deep-MOND systems (the Milky Way trims their boost by 7-15%) and NGC 2419 is internally dominated with a boost
      of only 1.3x.  What is really being tested is the framework's low-acceleration boost, not its EFE.
  (2) Pinned to the measured dispersions with ONE stellar M/L_V for all four clusters, the framework wants
      M/L_V = 0.76 +- 0.15 and Newton with no dark matter at all wants 2.14 +- 0.44, against a stellar-population
      value of 1.3-2.2.  Marginalised over a 0.15 dex M/L prior the framework is excluded at
      4.6 sigma (canonical) / 4.9 sigma (alt) -- real, but a 0.3 dex M/L systematic spans it.
  (3) Pal 3 goes the other way and is recorded: its 1.70 km/s from 22 stars needs a Newtonian M/L_V near 8 and sits
      comfortably on the framework.  Either it is a point for the framework or it is binary-inflated -- and if it is
      binary-inflated, so are the other three, which makes the framework's over-prediction worse.
  (4) The power and the lever sit in different clusters: NGC 2419 has 183 radial velocities and the smallest boost;
      Pal 14 has the largest boost and 16 stars.  That is why the published literature never closed this.
  (5) Nothing here is new physics; it reproduces Baumgardt+2005 / Jordi+2009 / Frank+2012 with the repo's own kernel
      and footings, and reproduces the Gentile+2010 counter-argument as well.
  (6) The liability is quoted at its SMALLEST.  Two systematics were identified and deliberately left uncorrected
      because both run in the framework's favour: unresolved binaries inflate every measured dispersion, and the
      three sparse clusters' single bins sit inside r_h,l where the dispersion is biased high.  Correcting the
      second at its maximum defensible size takes the over-prediction from 1.79x to 2.01x.  Every input number
      was re-fetched from Baumgardt's own parameter page and compared, and matches to machine precision.

RESULT: 15 checks, 0 FAIL   rc=0
